   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Elemental Composition of Pure Substances",
    "stem": "A pure compound contains only carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen. Elemental analysis shows it is 40.0% C, 6.7% H, and 53.3% O by mass, and its molar mass is 180 g/mol. What is the molecular formula of the compound?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "CH2O",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This stops at the empirical formula. CH2O has a mass of only 30 g/mol, which does not match the given molar mass of 180 g/mol, so it cannot be the molecular formula. Fix: Divide the given molar mass by the empirical formula mass and multiply every subscript by that whole number."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "C6HO8",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This divides the mass percentages directly by the smallest percentage (40.0 ÷ 6.7 ≈ 6, 53.3 ÷ 6.7 ≈ 8) without converting to moles. Mass ratios are not atom ratios because each element has a different molar mass. Fix: Convert each element's grams to moles using its molar mass before dividing by the smallest value."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "C6H6O6",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This treats hydrogen as H2 (2.0 g/mol), giving 3.3 mol H and a false 1:1:1 ratio. Within a compound, hydrogen is counted as atoms with molar mass 1.008 g/mol, so there are about 6.6 mol H, twice the moles of C. Fix: Use the atomic molar mass from the periodic table for every element, never a diatomic molecular mass."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "C6H12O6",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "In a 100 g sample: 40.0 g C ÷ 12.01 g/mol ≈ 3.33 mol, 6.7 g H ÷ 1.008 g/mol ≈ 6.6 mol, 53.3 g O ÷ 16.00 g/mol ≈ 3.33 mol. Dividing by 3.33 gives 1:2:1, so the empirical formula is CH2O (30 g/mol). Since 180 ÷ 30 = 6, every subscript is multiplied by 6."
     }
    ]
   },
